---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { REPO } from '../lib/catalog';
import { href } from '../lib/site';
---
<Base
  title="Privacy policy: axis"
  description="What the axis site and its page-view analytics collect, and what they do not."
  path="privacy/"
>
  <section class="band">
    <div class="container prose-wrap">
      <p class="crumb"><a href={href()}>axis</a></p>
      <div class="prose">
        <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
        <p>
          This page explains what the axis site collects when you visit it, and what it does not. axis is a public catalog of Agent eXperience
          Interfaces by bytesbybrandon, served from <a href={href()}>bytesbybrandon.github.io/axis</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Information we collect</h2>
        <p>
          The site has no accounts, no forms and no sign-in, and it asks for no name, email address or other personal details. The only visitor
          data comes from the page-view analytics described below, plus the routine request logs kept by GitHub Pages, which serves the site.
        </p>

        <h2>Site analytics</h2>
        <p>
          axis counts page views with Umami, open-source analytics software that the site owner hosts. GitHub Pages cannot proxy the tracker, so
          your browser loads the tracker script directly from
          <span class="break-all">https://umami-production-abb4.up.railway.app/script.js</span> and sends page-view data to that host. The data is
          stored in a database the site owner controls, not at an analytics vendor. It is not used for advertising and is not sold.
        </p>
        <p>
          Only visits on bytesbybrandon.github.io are counted, so localhost and other deployments are not. If your browser sends a Do Not Track
          signal, the tracker does not send anything. Page views are not linked to a name, email address or account, because the site has none.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Each page view records the page address (including any query string), the referrer, the page title, your browser and operating system,
            device type, screen size, language, and an approximate location (country, region and city) looked up from your IP address
          </li>
          <li>
            Umami's documentation says your IP address is used for that lookup and is never stored by Umami; GitHub Pages may still log it in
            routine request logs, as described above
          </li>
          <li>
            The tracker sets no cookies and writes nothing to your browser storage, and Umami's documentation says visitors are not tracked across
            websites
          </li>
          <li>
            Repeat visits are counted using a hash of values such as your IP address and browser user agent combined with a salt that rotates
            monthly by default, rather than a cookie or identifier stored on your device
          </li>
          <li>Self-hosted Umami keeps data until it is deleted and has no automatic expiry</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What we do not do</h2>
        <p>
          The site sets no cookies, runs no advertising tracker or session recorder, and shows no consent banner because there is nothing to consent
          to. Nothing collected here is sold or shared for advertising.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>This policy changes only when the site's data handling changes. The version on this page is the current one.</p>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>Questions about this policy or how the site handles data belong in an issue at <a href={REPO}>{REPO}</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
